\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{theorem:main}]
We prove the result by induction. Assume that the statement holds for $n$, we need to show it holds for $n+1$.

Firstly, consider the case when $n = 1$, the statement clearly holds because $P(1) = 1$. Now, assume that the statement holds for $n = k$, i.e., $P(k) = k^2$.

Now, consider the case $n = k+1$. Note that by the recursive definition of our construction, we have:
\begin{equation}
P(k+1) = P(k) + 2k + 1
\label{eq:inductive-step}
\end{equation}
Substituting the inductive hypothesis into equation \eqref{eq:inductive-step}, we obtain:
\begin{align}
P(k+1) &= k^2 + 2k + 1 \\
&= (k+1)^2
\end{align}
which concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

